+# WHICH STORE WEARS THE CROWN when two stores' winning rows cost exactly the SAME per unit (2026-09-19,
+# backlog I175, Brad's option A). This was `$byStore | Sort-Object unit_price`, and 5.1's Sort-Object is not
+# stable, so an exact cross-store tie handed cheapest_store, nomem_store AND the order of the whole `stores`
+# array to the sort's internals - the same defect Select-StoreWinner's key 4 exists to close one level down.
+# Measured on the live comparison-2026-09-17.json: 23 of 572 commodities (543 of which price two or more
+# stores) have rank 1 and rank 2 at an EXACTLY equal per_unit, and 26 have that tie between the first two
+# no-membership stores. Keys, in order:
+#   1. per-unit price  - unchanged and first, so no cell's price moves and no cheaper store loses a crown
+#   2. no membership   - a tie must not send a reader to a store they have to buy a membership to shop at.
+#                        It cannot make the board dearer: it only ever speaks between equal prices, and it
+#                        pulls cheapest_store toward nomem_store rather than away from it.
+#   3. store name      - a TOTAL order (store names are unique on a board row), so the crown is reproducible
+# THE RANK IS RETURNED WHOLE, not just its head: `stores` is emitted from it, so the published order of the
+# tied stores becomes reproducible too, and `nomem_store` is its first non-member row.
+function Select-CrossStoreRank($winners) {
+  @($winners |
+    Sort-Object @{Expression = { $_.unit_price }},
+                @{Expression = { if ($_.membership) { 1 } else { 0 } }},
+                @{Expression = { [string]$_.store }})
+}
